From 7c5a26bfc21631f5ff5fa4141f7b5cfefc3b2cd1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 05:00:40 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] Outfits: the fabric repair's cut search holds the GIL far
 less

The seam repair finds its minimum-error cut on pairs of rows (moving up
to two columns a pair), and a closed cut (the second pass, which must
keep the first pass's tiling) is the cheapest one that starts where the
cheapest open one does, instead of the best of three starts. That loop
over rows is the longest stretch of Python a fabric job runs on its
worker thread, and it now has about a third of the steps, each with
fewer numpy calls. The repair gives the same results on the synthetic
textures: 46 of 48 cut-out ones below 1.25 (the same two smoothest
left), and the six of the docs preview score as before.

Measured with a main thread that ticks every 5 ms while a worker repairs
swatches nonstop, in half-second windows alternating with an idle worker,
old and new code run in turn at the same load: the 99th percentile of
the ticks' lateness went from 36 ms to about 1 ms, the worst tick from
72-75 ms to 3-9 ms (5-7 ms with the worker idle). The README gives these
figures and the timings of the score, the light and the repair again.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01B99J6LuDFd7VnzjjcAYtWv
